  <section>
    <h1>About me</h1>
    <p>
      I'm Adithya M D, an engineer in Bengaluru.
    </p>
    <p>
      I work at the NeuRonICS Lab at the Indian Institute of Science, where I
      build machine learning systems that run on microcontrollers. The models I
      work with listen to audio, read radar and see through event cameras, and
      they do it on hardware with a few kilobytes of memory. Some of them are
      out in the field right now, counting insects for entomologists in India
      and Australia.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before IISc I co-founded IXE Tronics, where we built Culti7, a moisture
      meter for the cashew processing industry. It has been in daily use since
      2021, in India and in West Africa. It taught me more about engineering
      than anything else I have done, mostly by breaking in ways no lab ever
      prepares you for.
    </p>
    <p>
      Teaching runs alongside all of it. I have been a course assistant at IISc
      for a graduate course on hardware acceleration and optimisation for
      machine learning, a teaching assistant for NPTEL's Mathematical
      Foundations for Machine Learning, and an instructor at TalentSprint.
      These days I teach computer organisation and architecture.
    </p>
    <p>
      The common thread is that I like to start from first principles and build
      up. This site is where that habit leaves a trail.
    </p>
    <p class="say-hello">
      Write to me at <a href="mailto:amdadithya@gmail.com">amdadithya@gmail.com</a>
      or find me on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/adithya-md/">LinkedIn</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

